Tests for save-session-on-change.patch, restore-window-placement.patch and restore-screens.patch, together with
patches/gtk4/ and patches/mutter/, save-toplevels-on-change.patch included, run by hand and in GitLab CI, see
.gitlab-ci.yml.

Ptyxis session restore: run-ptyxis-session.sh and ptyxis_session.py
    Checks that Ptyxis keeps its session up to date while it runs, so that after a crash it restores its windows
    under the names mutter placed them by, each tab in its directory and with its screen, and that closing the last
    window keeps the tabs but not the screens. Starts a headless mutter with one virtual monitor in a D-Bus session of
    its own and runs Ptyxis three times in it, with GSettings in memory, so that its defaults apply, and with a home
    directory and XDG directories of its own, so that it runs next to your own Ptyxis without touching its session.

    The first run opens a window with two tabs and a second window with one, each tab in a directory of its own, and
    once Ptyxis saved its session and GTK its window state, mutter and Ptyxis are ended with SIGKILL, as in a crash
    of the machine, and a new mutter is started. The second run restores both windows, each tab with the screen of
    the first run above the output of a new shell, and then closes both windows with Alt+F4. The third run restores
    the window closed last, with new shells and without screens. Without patches/mutter/save-toplevels-on-change.patch
    the new mutter knows neither the session nor the windows, and the restore checks of the second run fail.

    Each run starts Ptyxis in another way: the first from its command line, the second as a D-Bus service asked to
    activate its new-window action, as GNOME Shell starts it from the dock and the overview, and the third as a D-Bus
    service asked to activate itself. Without restore-session-on-new-window-action.patch the second run opens a blank
    window instead of restoring the session.

    The test presses keys through mutter's remote desktop interface. Without a keyboard, mutter gives no window the
    keyboard focus, and GTK saves its window state only while a window of the application has it.

    Mutter's binary is only in the mutter package, and Ptyxis is tested without installing it, so both are extracted,
    mutter from the local repository and Ptyxis from the build to test. Ptyxis runs with the installed GTK, which has
    to be the one from patches/gtk4/, and the installed ptyxis-agent:

      mkdir /tmp/mutter-root /tmp/ptyxis-root
      for p in mutter libmutter-18-0; do dpkg-deb -x /usr/local/lib/debs/${p}_*.deb /tmp/mutter-root; done
      dpkg-deb -x /usr/local/lib/debs/ptyxis_*.deb /tmp/ptyxis-root
      ./run-ptyxis-session.sh /tmp/mutter-root /tmp/ptyxis-root /tmp/ptyxis-session-test

    The shells read a .bashrc the test writes, which moves each shell of the first run into a directory of its own and
    prints "first run, tab N", and in later runs prints "second run in" and the directory it started in. The first
    tab prints nothing for its first 5 seconds, so that Ptyxis saves its session once while no tab has a screen, which
    once kept all later screens from being saved. ptyxis-agent starts the shells through systemd-run --user when it
    finds systemd-run, which needs the user's systemd manager in the runtime directory the test replaces, so a
    systemd-run which fails comes first on PATH, and the agent starts the shells directly. The checks
    read Ptyxis's session state and screens, GTK's window state, and what Ptyxis sent and received over the session
    management protocol, which the script prints, followed by one line for each check, and finally "RESULT: OK", and
    exits with 0. A tab which is not the selected one of its window starts its shell only once it is shown, so in the
    second run it shows just its screen of the first run.

    The full WAYLAND_DEBUG output of each run is in the output directory as run1.log, run2.log and run3.log, and the
    output of the two mutters as mutter1.log and mutter2.log. The D-Bus and portal messages printed on the way, and
    Ptyxis's lost connection when mutter ends, are expected.
